@*
    The Settings page's Appearance tab: pick a theme from ThemeCatalog.Grouped, and set the Human's
    own Avatar.

    Two sections, not one control. The first is the Theme picker: a theme carries exactly one
    palette and its own ThemeMode, so picking a theme picks light or dark with it - there is no
    separate light/dark control any more, and so no way to ask for "Solarized Dark, in light mode"
    and be shown a different theme's palette instead (docs/adr/0017-a-theme-is-a-palette-not-a-pair.md).

    The second section edits the Human's Avatar. A Teammate's Avatar is edited on its own
    TeammateCard, because a Teammate has a Persona file for that card to hold open; the Human has no
    Persona file and therefore no Teammate card, so this tab - the one place a per-installation
    display choice already lived - is where the Human's Avatar is edited instead. It reuses Phase
    5.1's exact editor: the MudRadioGroup, the Immediate label field trimmed through
    Avatar.TrimLabel, the MudFileUpload validated the same way (Size against AvatarImage.MaxBytes,
    then AvatarImage.SniffExtension), and the MudColorPicker bound to Text and never Value - see that
    control's own comment below for the trap. The one deliberate difference: this tab has no Save
    button, so every change here writes to avatars.json immediately - the same way choosing a theme
    above applies immediately - rather than buffering until a Save the way TeammateCard's editor does.
    There is no Cancel to protect here, so there is no reason to hold an upload's bytes back either.

    "Appearance" does not widen to cover this. appearance.json still holds only the selected Theme
    id and nothing else; the Human's Avatar is edited on this tab but stored in avatars.json, a
    different file with a different owner (AvatarStore, not AppearanceStore).

    A MudList rather than a MudSelect: MudBlazor 9 has no MudSelectItemGroup, so a MudSelect cannot
    render the Light / Dark / High contrast headings this picker is grouped by. The list also shows
    every theme at once, which is what a theme picker wants - the same shape VS Code's own picker has,
    and the catalog these themes came from.

    The grouping is ThemeCatalog.Grouped's, computed once there rather than here, because
    ThemeCatalog.BuiltIn's ORDER is load-bearing - three call sites read BuiltIn[0] as "the default
    theme" - so the picker must never be tempted to reorder the catalog to group it.

    MudBlazor's MudThemeProvider lives in the render tree (MainLayout.razor), so choosing a theme here
    applies immediately through that component's own parameters - no page reload, no <head> to
    rewrite, no JavaScript interop of this component's own.

    Subscribes to AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged the same way Settings.razor subscribes to
    HookStore.HooksChanged, and to AvatarStore.AvatarsChanged for the same reason: both stores are
    singletons, so a hand-edit to appearance.json or avatars.json (or a save from another browser
    tab) must repaint this tab without a restart, and rules.md's "unsubscribe in Dispose" reasoning
    for RoomEvents applies here for the same reason - a leaked subscription to a singleton never
    dies.
*@

@using Agency.Huddle.App.Appearance
@using Agency.Huddle.App.Avatars
@using Agency.Huddle.App.Themes
@using Microsoft.Extensions.Options
@using System.Globalization
@implements IDisposable
@inject AppearanceStore AppearanceStore
@inject AvatarStore Avatars
@inject IOptions<TeamOptions> Options

<p class="teammates-intro">
    Pick a theme. Each theme is either a light or a dark one, so choosing it sets the application's
    light or dark colours too.
</p>

<MudList T="string"
         Class="theme-picker"
         SelectionMode="SelectionMode.SingleSelection"
         SelectedValue="@this.SelectedThemeId"
         SelectedValueChanged="this.OnThemeChangedAsync"
         aria-label="Theme">
    @foreach (var grouping in ThemeCatalog.Grouped)
    {
        <MudListSubheader Class="theme-picker-heading">@grouping.Heading</MudListSubheader>
        @foreach (var theme in grouping.Themes)
        {
            <MudListItem T="string" Value="@theme.Id" Text="@theme.Label" />
        }
    }
</MudList>

<p class="settings-theme-credit">
    Every theme except Huddle Light and Huddle Dark is one of the colour themes
    bundled with Visual Studio Code, mapped onto this application's palette. Visual Studio Code and
    its default themes are Microsoft's, under the MIT licence; Solarized is Ethan Schoonover's and
    Monokai is Wimer Hazenberg's.
</p>

<p class="settings-hooks-file-path">
    The selection is stored at <code>@this.AppearanceStore.FilePath</code>. The file does not exist
    until you save a choice here, so it being absent is expected, not a bug.
</p>

@* See the file-level comment for why this section exists: the Human has no Persona file, so this is
   where their Avatar is edited instead of on a TeammateCard. Every control below saves on its own
   change - there is no Save button on this tab - which is the one deliberate difference from
   TeammateCard's identical-looking editor. *@
<MudPaper Outlined="true" Class="pa-3 mt-4">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.overline">Your avatar</MudText>

    <MudStack Row="true" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Class="mb-2">
        <TeammateAvatar Name="@this.HumanName" Avatar="this.CurrentAvatar" Size="Size.Large" />
    </MudStack>

    <MudRadioGroup T="AvatarChoice" Value="this.avatarChoice" ValueChanged="this.OnAvatarChoiceChanged" Class="mb-2">
        <MudRadio T="AvatarChoice" Value="AvatarChoice.Initials">Initials of the name</MudRadio>
        <MudRadio T="AvatarChoice" Value="AvatarChoice.Label">A short label</MudRadio>
        <MudRadio T="AvatarChoice" Value="AvatarChoice.Image">An image</MudRadio>
    </MudRadioGroup>

    @if (this.avatarChoice == AvatarChoice.Label)
    {
        @* Immediate="true", and trimming happens once inside CurrentAvatar's own Avatar.TrimLabel call
           rather than here - the same reasoning TeammateCard's identical control gives, except that
           OnAvatarLabelChanged below also saves on every keystroke, since this tab has no Save button.
           No MaxLength: it truncates by UTF-16 index, which can cut a surrogate pair in half. Counter
           gives the same affordance without that defect, sized from Avatar.MaxLabelTextElements so the
           two can never drift apart. *@
        <MudTextField T="string" Label="Label" Value="@this.avatarLabel" ValueChanged="this.OnAvatarLabelChanged"
                      Immediate="true" Counter="@Avatar.MaxLabelTextElements"
                      HelperText="Shown instead of initials." />
    }

    @if (this.avatarChoice == AvatarChoice.Image)
    {
        <MudFileUpload T="IBrowserFile" Accept=".png,.jpg,.jpeg,.webp" MaximumFileCount="1" FilesChanged="this.OnAvatarFileChangedAsync">
            <CustomContent Context="fileUpload">
                <MudButton Variant="Variant.Outlined" StartIcon="@Icons.Material.Filled.Upload" OnClick="@(() => fileUpload.OpenFilePickerAsync())">
                    Choose an image
                </MudButton>
            </CustomContent>
        </MudFileUpload>

        @if (this.avatarImage is not null)
        {
            <MudStack Row="true" AlignItems="AlignItems.Center" Spacing="2" Class="mt-2">
                <MudText Typo="Typo.body2">@this.avatarImage</MudText>
                <MudButton Variant="Variant.Text" Size="Size.Small" OnClick="this.RemoveAvatarImage">Remove image</MudButton>
            </MudStack>
        }
    }

    @if (this.avatarError is not null)
    {
        <MudAlert Severity="Severity.Error" Dense="true" Class="mt-2">@this.avatarError</MudAlert>
    }

    @* THE TRAP: MudColorPicker.Value is a non-null MudColor - binding it would materialise a
       hard-coded hex colour the moment this tab first renders, permanently divorced from the Theme,
       just from opening this tab with no background ever chosen. Text is a string? and is exactly the
       value this component reads and writes to avatars.json, so binding IT instead keeps "no
       background chosen" (null) expressible - see avatarBackground's own remarks and
       docs/agencyteam/traps.md. *@
    <MudColorPicker Label="Background" ColorPickerMode="ColorPickerMode.HEX" ShowAlpha="false"
                     Text="@this.avatarBackground" TextChanged="this.OnAvatarBackgroundTextChanged" Class="mt-2" />
    <MudButton Variant="Variant.Text" Size="Size.Small" OnClick="this.UseThemeColour">Use the theme colour</MudButton>
</MudPaper>

<p class="settings-hooks-file-path">
    Your avatar is stored at <code>@this.Avatars.FilePath</code>, under your Name
    (<code>@this.HumanName</code>) - a different file from the Theme selection above.
</p>

@code {
    /// <summary>The theme id shown selected: the store's current selection, or the catalog's default when nothing is selected yet.</summary>
    private string SelectedThemeId => this.AppearanceStore.Current.ThemeId ?? ThemeCatalog.BuiltIn[0].Id;

    // Editor-only working state for the Human's avatar editor - the same three fields
    // TeammateCard.SeedAvatarEditorState seeds, minus the pending-upload buffer that editor needs and
    // this one does not (see the file-level comment: no Save button here means no Cancel to protect,
    // so an upload writes and saves immediately instead of waiting in a buffer). avatarChoice is
    // DERIVED from "avatar" only at seed time (see SeedAvatarEditorState) rather than stored, exactly
    // like TeammateCard's own copy of this field.
    private AvatarChoice avatarChoice;
    private string avatarLabel = string.Empty;
    private string? avatarImage;
    private string? avatarBackground;
    private string? avatarError;

    /// <summary>
    /// The Human's configured Name (<see cref="TeamOptions.HumanName"/>) - the key this tab's Avatar
    /// is saved under in <c>avatars.json</c>, and the same Name that later appears as
    /// <see cref="Agency.Huddle.Contracts.ChatMessage.SenderName"/> on the Human's own messages.
    /// </summary>
    private string HumanName => this.Options.Value.HumanName;

    /// <summary>
    /// The single place <see cref="avatarChoice"/>, <see cref="avatarLabel"/>, <see cref="avatarImage"/>
    /// and <see cref="avatarBackground"/> are projected back into an <see cref="Avatars.Avatar"/> - both
    /// for the live preview <see cref="TeammateAvatar"/> renders above and for what every handler below
    /// saves. Mirrors <c>TeammateCard.CurrentAvatar</c> exactly.
    /// </summary>
    private Avatar CurrentAvatar => new(
        this.avatarChoice == AvatarChoice.Label ? Avatar.TrimLabel(this.avatarLabel) : null,
        this.avatarChoice == AvatarChoice.Image ? this.avatarImage : null,
        this.avatarBackground);

    // Set immediately before every SaveAvatar call below and consumed by OnAvatarsChanged, on the
    // same thread: AvatarStore.Save raises AvatarsChanged synchronously, from inside Save itself, so
    // by the time SaveAvatar returns the echo has already been read and cleared - no race. Without
    // this, choosing "A short label" before typing anything saves Avatar.None (nothing is set yet),
    // AvatarsChanged fires, and OnAvatarsChanged's own SeedAvatarEditorState call would read that
    // still-empty entry back and silently flip avatarChoice back to Initials - the very choice the
    // Human just made, undone by its own save. This flag is what tells "my own echo" apart from a
    // genuine external edit, which still must re-seed (see OnAvatarsChanged).
    private bool savedLocally;

    /// <summary>Subscribes to <see cref="AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged"/> and <see cref="AvatarStore.AvatarsChanged"/> - see the file-level comment for why this matters.</summary>
    protected override void OnInitialized()
    {
        this.AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged += this.OnAppearanceChanged;
        this.Avatars.AvatarsChanged += this.OnAvatarsChanged;
        this.SeedAvatarEditorState();
    }

    /// <summary>Unsubscribes from <see cref="AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged"/> and <see cref="AvatarStore.AvatarsChanged"/> - see the file-level comment for why this matters.</summary>
    public void Dispose()
    {
        this.AppearanceStore.AppearanceChanged -= this.OnAppearanceChanged;
        this.Avatars.AvatarsChanged -= this.OnAvatarsChanged;
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// (Re-)seeds every avatar-editor local field from <see cref="AvatarStore.Get(string)"/>, called
    /// once from <see cref="OnInitialized"/> and again from <see cref="OnAvatarsChanged"/> for a
    /// genuine external change - never for this component's own save, which <see cref="OnAvatarsChanged"/>
    /// filters out via <see cref="savedLocally"/> before this is reached. <see cref="avatarChoice"/> is
    /// derived from which of <see cref="Avatars.Avatar.Image"/> and <see cref="Avatars.Avatar.Label"/>
    /// is set - the same Image-then-Label-then-initials precedence <see cref="TeammateAvatar"/> renders
    /// by - rather than read from any stored discriminator, because none is ever written to disk.
    /// Mirrors <c>TeammateCard.SeedAvatarEditorState</c>.
    /// </summary>
    private void SeedAvatarEditorState()
    {
        var avatar = this.Avatars.Get(this.HumanName);
        this.avatarChoice = avatar.Image is not null
            ? AvatarChoice.Image
            : avatar.Label is not null
                ? AvatarChoice.Label
                : AvatarChoice.Initials;
        this.avatarLabel = avatar.Label ?? string.Empty;
        this.avatarImage = avatar.Image;
        this.avatarBackground = avatar.Background;
        this.avatarError = null;
    }

    /// <summary>Saves <see cref="CurrentAvatar"/> under <see cref="HumanName"/> and arms <see cref="savedLocally"/> first - every handler below calls this instead of <see cref="AvatarStore.Save(string, Avatar)"/> directly, so none of them can forget to arm it.</summary>
    private void SaveAvatar()
    {
        this.savedLocally = true;
        this.Avatars.Save(this.HumanName, this.CurrentAvatar);
    }

    /// <summary>Switches the three-way avatar choice and saves immediately - see the file-level comment for why this tab has no buffered "unsaved change" to hold instead.</summary>
    /// <param name="value">The radio button just selected.</param>
    private void OnAvatarChoiceChanged(AvatarChoice value)
    {
        this.avatarChoice = value;
        this.avatarError = null;
        this.SaveAvatar();
    }

    /// <summary>Stores the Label box's newly typed text and saves immediately, once per keystroke - trimming happens once, inside <see cref="CurrentAvatar"/>'s own <see cref="Avatar.TrimLabel(string?)"/> call, not here.</summary>
    /// <param name="value">The Label box's newly typed value.</param>
    private void OnAvatarLabelChanged(string value)
    {
        this.avatarLabel = value;
        this.avatarError = null;
        this.SaveAvatar();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Validates an uploaded avatar image and, on success, writes it through
    /// <see cref="AvatarStore.WriteImage(ReadOnlySpan{byte}, string)"/> and saves immediately - unlike
    /// <c>TeammateCard.OnAvatarFileChangedAsync</c>, which only buffers the bytes: see the file-level
    /// comment for why there is nothing here to defer to a later Save.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="file">The single file <see cref="MudFileUpload{T}"/> just selected.</param>
    private async Task OnAvatarFileChangedAsync(IBrowserFile file)
    {
        ArgumentNullException.ThrowIfNull(file);

        this.avatarError = null;

        // Checked before opening the stream at all - fast, and gives a better message than the
        // IOException OpenReadStream's own maxAllowedSize guard would throw for the same file.
        if (file.Size > AvatarImage.MaxBytes)
        {
            this.avatarError = string.Create(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, $"That file is larger than {AvatarImage.MaxBytes / 1000} KB.");
            return;
        }

        byte[] bytes;
        try
        {
            await using var stream = file.OpenReadStream(AvatarImage.MaxBytes, CancellationToken.None);
            using MemoryStream buffer = new();
            await stream.CopyToAsync(buffer, CancellationToken.None);
            bytes = buffer.ToArray();
        }
        catch (IOException)
        {
            // A lying Size (or a stream that grew mid-read) still trips OpenReadStream's own
            // maxAllowedSize guard - same message as the fast check above, since it is the same
            // fact from the user's point of view.
            this.avatarError = string.Create(CultureInfo.InvariantCulture, $"That file is larger than {AvatarImage.MaxBytes / 1000} KB.");
            return;
        }

        var extension = AvatarImage.SniffExtension(bytes);
        if (extension is null)
        {
            this.avatarError = "That file is not a PNG, JPEG or WebP image.";
            return;
        }

        this.avatarImage = this.Avatars.WriteImage(bytes, extension);
        this.SaveAvatar();
    }

    /// <summary>Clears the chosen image and saves immediately, so <see cref="CurrentAvatar"/> falls back to initials until a new file is chosen.</summary>
    private void RemoveAvatarImage()
    {
        this.avatarImage = null;
        this.avatarError = null;
        this.SaveAvatar();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// THE TRAP: bound to <see cref="MudColorPicker.Text"/>, never <see cref="MudColorPicker.Value"/> -
    /// see the markup comment beside the control for why. Blank input (the Clear action, or a user who
    /// deletes the hex text) maps to <see langword="null"/>, meaning "the surface's own default", never
    /// the empty string - <see cref="Avatar.Background"/>'s own contract. Saves immediately.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="value">The colour picker's newly typed or picked text, or <see langword="null"/>/blank.</param>
    private void OnAvatarBackgroundTextChanged(string? value)
    {
        this.avatarBackground = string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(value) ? null : value;
        this.avatarError = null;
        this.SaveAvatar();
    }

    /// <summary>Explicitly clears <see cref="avatarBackground"/> back to <see langword="null"/> - the Theme's own colour - and saves immediately.</summary>
    private void UseThemeColour()
    {
        this.avatarBackground = null;
        this.avatarError = null;
        this.SaveAvatar();
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Repaints this tab, and re-seeds the avatar editor, whenever <see cref="AvatarStore.AvatarsChanged"/>
    /// fires for a genuine external edit - a hand-edit to <c>avatars.json</c>, or a save from another
    /// browser tab. Consumes and skips <see cref="savedLocally"/> first: <see cref="SaveAvatar"/> raises
    /// this same event synchronously as part of this component's OWN save, and re-seeding from that
    /// echo would read back whatever was just written - which, for a choice with nothing behind it yet
    /// (picking "A short label" before typing anything saves <see cref="Avatar.None"/>), is nothing,
    /// silently reverting the very choice the Human just made. See <see cref="savedLocally"/>'s own
    /// remarks.
    /// </summary>
    private void OnAvatarsChanged()
    {
        if (this.savedLocally)
        {
            this.savedLocally = false;
            return;
        }

        _ = this.DispatchAsync(() =>
        {
            this.SeedAvatarEditorState();
            this.StateHasChanged();
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        });
    }

    /// <summary>
    /// Saves the newly picked theme id. A null reaches here when MudList clears its selection, which
    /// SingleSelection does when the selected item is clicked again; the store reads that as "no
    /// theme selected" and the catalog's default applies, which is the right answer either way.
    /// </summary>
    /// <param name="themeId">The theme id the Human just picked.</param>
    private Task OnThemeChangedAsync(string? themeId)
    {
        this.AppearanceStore.Save(themeId);
        return Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    private void OnAppearanceChanged()
    {
        _ = this.DispatchAsync(() =>
        {
            this.StateHasChanged();
            return Task.CompletedTask;
        });
    }

    // Mirrors Settings.razor's DispatchAsync exactly: a store event can still fire while this
    // component's circuit is winding down, and both exceptions mean there is nothing left to repaint.
    private async Task DispatchAsync(Func<Task> action)
    {
        try
        {
            await this.InvokeAsync(action);
        }
        catch (ObjectDisposedException)
        {
        }
        catch (InvalidOperationException)
        {
        }
    }
}
